    <p>Planning a tiny home in Texas raises the same questions every time. Can I legally live in it? Do I need a permit? What will it cost once land, utilities and site work are added? Should I build on a trailer or a foundation? And how do I tell a good builder from one who will leave me with a half-finished shell?</p>
    <p>The Cowboy Tiny Homes team wrote these guides to answer those questions honestly. Texas has no single statewide tiny home law. Rules come from your city, your county, your homeowners association and the way your home is built, so the right answer usually starts with "it depends on where and what." Each guide below explains what it depends on, gives you real numbers to plan with, and shows you what to check before you spend money.</p>
    <p>If you are just starting out, read the <a href="/guides/tiny-house-laws-texas/">Texas tiny house laws guide</a> first. Whether your home counts as a house, an accessory dwelling unit or a recreational vehicle decides almost everything that follows: permits, financing, insurance and where you can park it. Then use the <a href="/guides/cost-to-build-a-tiny-house-in-texas/">cost guide</a> and our <a href="/tools/tiny-home-cost-calculator/">tiny home cost calculator</a> to set a realistic budget.</p>
